WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.900
Thejeshwaar Sathishkumar
I build models for noisy signals.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.900
From outside, a wearable records four signals.
Brain, heart, muscle and movement. The obvious plan: fuse them.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.900
Three of them counted for nothing.
I looked inside the model. The fusion weights on heart, muscle and movement were 0.000. Exactly zero.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.900
And it was barely looking.
It was reading 2 s of each 17 s window.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:32.900
So I rebuilt it, one stage at a time.
Then tested it on patients it never saw in training. Under review at npj Digital Medicine.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.900
Then I audited my own best number.
An ADHD classifier of mine had scored this. The epoch had been chosen on the test fold.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:47.900
A stopwatch beat chance.
Recording length alone, with no EEG in it, beat chance. So I rebuilt the evaluation, subject-independent.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.900
Two more cases, held to the same standard.
A detector that had to beat “always yes” first. Synthetic transactions with scripted life events: F1 above 0.75 on 3 of 4 rare events, on synthetic data.
Can phase survive a quantum circuit? Exploratory, under review at Frontiers in AI. No quantum advantage claimed.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:01.900
Every result gets the same treatment.
I build models for noisy signals. Then I find out whether to believe them.
